      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitehouse%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village grew around a tavern called the White House, a name travelers adopted in the 1700s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitehouse%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Stones from the original tavern can be seen along the retaining wall of the Daughters of the American Revolution cemetery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitehouse%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Whitehouse–Mechanicsville Historic District, covering sites in both villages, was added to the National Register of Historic Places in 2015.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitehouse%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1722 Abraham Van Horne purchased 490 acres along Rockaway Creek and built a grist mill and saw mill that helped launch the settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitehouse%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The community once stretched along the Jersey Turnpike, now Route 22 and Old Route 28, the main street of its era.",
